%I A400486 #8 Oct 04 2026 18:29:33
%S A400486 0,0,0,1,4,11,29,65,145,305,641,1317,2733,5565,11397,23181,47181,
%T A400486 95589,193901,392157,793277,1601597,3234269,6522141,13151005,26496189,
%U A400486 53370717,107443197,216253629,435078205
%N A400486 Number of positive integers m < 2^n - 1 such that the Collatz trajectories of m and m+1 meet within n steps, after equal numbers of halving steps and before either reaches 1.
%C A400486 The Collatz map is f(x) = x/2 for even x and 3*x+1 for odd x (A006370). Here m is counted if f^j(m) = f^j(m+1) for some j <= n, the two trajectories contain the same number of even terms among their first j terms, and 1 does not occur among f^i(m) and f^i(m+1) for 0 <= i <= j. In the terminology of Gao (1993), m and m+1 then coalesce.
%C A400486 If m and m+1 coalesce and m reaches 1, then m and m+1 take the same number of steps to reach 1 (A006577) and the same number of halving steps (A006666), so m is in A078417. Conversely, for m in A078417 the pair coalesces exactly when the two numbers of halving steps agree; Gao asked whether they always do, and they do for all m < 2^24.
%C A400486 Gao tabulated a(n)/2^n to three decimals for 4 <= n <= 24 (Table 3, from 0.063 to 0.389), proved that a(n)/2^n is nondecreasing, that is, a(n+1) >= 2*a(n) (his Corollary 2, from his Lemma 2), and conjectured that a(n)/2^n -> 1.
%C A400486 Lagarias's annotated bibliography records the conjecture in another form: the set of m such that f^k(m) = f^k(m+1) for some k <= log_2(m) has natural density 1. A meeting within log_2(m) steps is always a coalescence in Gao's sense, so this form implies a(n)/2^n -> 1.
%C A400486 The ratio a(n)/2^n grows very slowly: it is 0.389 for n = 24 and 0.400 for n = 28.
%H A400486 Guo-Gang Gao, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-365X(93)90240-T">On consecutive numbers of the same height in the Collatz problem</a>, Discrete Math. 112 (1993), 261-267.
%H A400486 Jeffrey C. Lagarias, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0309224">The 3x+1 problem: An annotated bibliography (1963-1999) (sorted by author)</a>, arXiv:math/0309224 [math.NT], 2003-2011 (entry 68).
%F A400486 a(n+1) >= 2*a(n).
%e A400486 a(4) = 1: the only m < 15 counted is 12, since 12 -> 6 -> 3 -> 10 and 13 -> 40 -> 20 -> 10 meet after 3 steps with 2 halving steps each.
%e A400486 a(5) = 4: the m < 31 counted are 12, 18, 20 and 28; for instance 18 -> 9 -> 28 -> 14 -> 7 -> 22 and 19 -> 58 -> 29 -> 88 -> 44 -> 22 meet after 5 steps with 3 halving steps each. Although 14 and 15 take the same number of steps to reach 1, m = 14 is first counted at n = 9, since the trajectories of 14 and 15 first meet at 40, after 9 steps.
%o A400486 (Python)
%o A400486 def f(x): return x//2 if x % 2 == 0 else 3*x+1
%o A400486 def counted(m, n):
%o A400486     x, y, hx, hy = m, m+1, 0, 0
%o A400486     for j in range(n+1):
%o A400486         if x == 1 or y == 1: return False
%o A400486         if x == y: return hx == hy
%o A400486         hx += x % 2 == 0
%o A400486         hy += y % 2 == 0
%o A400486         x, y = f(x), f(y)
%o A400486     return False
%o A400486 def a(n): return sum(counted(m, n) for m in range(2**n - 1))
%o A400486 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 17)])
%Y A400486 Cf. A006370, A006577, A006666, A078417, A078441, A398792, A400487.
%K A400486 nonn,more,new
%O A400486 1,5
